# decision One Hackage export adapter supports Cabal and Stack projects

The adapter ecosystem is `hackage`. `stack.yaml` selects the Stack variant; otherwise
`cabal.project` selects Cabal. Cabal wiring owns a delimited `source-repository-package` stanza
whose `tag` is `LockedDependency.Commit`. Stack wiring owns a delimited `extra-deps` git item
whose `commit` is the same exact commit. Both native forms carry an export subdirectory.

# invariant Haskell dependency blocks are precisely addressed

The package export name appears only in comment delimiters around the owned stanza/item. Human
Cabal fields and Stack entries outside those delimiters remain byte-for-byte unchanged.
When Stack has no `extra-deps`, its created header is marked and removed together with the last
owned item rather than leaving an empty project field. If the dependency named by the created
header is removed first, ownership transfers to another managed item so the YAML list remains valid.

# test Hackage adapter

- Cabal and Stack each have fixture/golden, idempotence, drift, and unwire coverage.
- Drift checks the normalized Git URL and locked commit in either variant.

# decision native materialization

`Pull` checks the saved tool before editing and then runs `cabal build all` for Cabal or
`stack build` for Stack. This resolves and materializes the dependency even when the owned block
was already current. Removal edits the owned block and asks the same saved manager for a dry-run
project plan, updating Stack's native lock without compiling consumer code; it never switches
variants. Offline `Inspect` requires Stack's exact native lock commit plus `.stack-work`, or for
Cabal the project plan plus an exact-commit Git checkout under `dist-newstyle/src`; missing or
stale manager evidence is repairable by Pull.
